name: moving data
boss: Plaintext
flavor: Every byte you send bare, I already have.
brief: wget fetches over http. nc opens a raw tcp connection and hands it to
brief: you as stdin and stdout, which is how you speak any text protocol by
brief: hand. The older ftp and tftp clients are here because the machines you
brief: will meet in a bad week are often older than you are.
brief:
brief: udhcpc is the first step when nothing handed you an address: it asks
brief: the network for one. telnet is nc with a protocol bolted on and no
brief: encryption anywhere -- fine for poking at a port, never for a login.
brief: ftpget and ftpput are one-shot transfers with no session to drive.
brief:
brief: This build of nc can connect out but cannot listen -- server mode is
brief: compiled out on purpose. xos opens no doors it was not asked to.
---
setup: file.txt is served over plain http below -- only the host and the
setup: flag change from here on.
q: {keep file.txt's own name pulling it from @host@|don't rename it -- grab file.txt from @host@|leave the name alone, fetching file.txt from @host@}, with wget
a: wget http://@host@/file.txt
why: fixture-cost
ref: wget
teach: wget
hint: wget with no -O saves the file under the url's own basename
---
q: {pick out.txt as your own name for file.txt from @host@|choose out.txt as the name, saving file.txt from @host@|rename it out.txt, pulling file.txt from @host@}, with wget
a: wget -O out.txt http://@host@/file.txt
why: fixture-cost
ref: wget
teach: wget -O
hint: -O picks the output filename yourself, instead of using the url's
---
q: {stay quiet fetching file.txt from @host@|silence the output grabbing file.txt from @host@|no progress noise pulling file.txt from @host@}, with wget
a: wget -q http://@host@/file.txt
why: fixture-cost
ref: wget
teach: wget -q
hint: -q silences wget's normal progress output
---
q: {pick up file.txt's stalled download from @host@|don't restart -- resume file.txt from @host@|continue file.txt's interrupted pull from @host@}, with wget
a: wget -c http://@host@/file.txt
why: fixture-cost
ref: wget
teach: wget -c
hint: -c picks up a partial download where it left off
---
q: {confirm file.txt is really there on @host@|check file.txt exists, no download, on @host@|make sure file.txt is there without pulling it from @host@}, with wget
a: wget --spider http://@host@/file.txt
why: fixture-cost
ref: wget
teach: wget --spider
hint: --spider checks the url without saving anything
---
q: {watch the headers fetching file.txt from @host@|see what headers come back pulling file.txt from @host@|check the response headers on file.txt from @host@}, with wget
a: wget -S http://@host@/file.txt
why: fixture-cost
ref: wget
teach: wget -S
hint: -S prints the server's response headers as they arrive
---
q: {fetch file.txt from @host@ using header 'X-Token: abc'|send header 'X-Token: abc' fetching file.txt from @host@|get file.txt from @host@ with header 'X-Token: abc'}, with wget
a: wget --header 'X-Token: abc' http://@host@/file.txt
why: fixture-cost
ref: wget
teach: wget --header
hint: --header adds one arbitrary header line to the request
---
q: {POST a=1 to @host@/submit instead of fetching|send a=1 to @host@/submit with a post, not a get|push a=1 to @host@/submit rather than downloading}, with wget
a: wget --post-data 'a=1' http://@host@/submit
why: fixture-cost
ref: wget
teach: wget --post-data
hint: --post-data sends the string as the request body over POST
---
q: {land file.txt in @dir@, pulling it from @host@|drop file.txt into @dir@ fetching from @host@|save file.txt into @dir@ instead, from @host@}, with wget
a: wget -P @dir@ http://@host@/file.txt
why: fixture-cost
ref: wget
teach: wget -P
hint: -P sets the directory the download is saved into
---
q: {give up on file.txt after 10 silent seconds from @host@|cap the wait on file.txt at 10s from @host@|abandon file.txt if @host@ stays quiet 10s}, with wget
a: wget -T 10 http://@host@/file.txt
why: fixture-cost
ref: wget
teach: wget -T
hint: -T caps how long wget waits for the server before giving up
---
q: {with wget, log file.txt's fetch to wget.log, from @host@|save file.txt's log to wget.log with wget, from @host@|track file.txt's pull in wget.log with wget, from @host@}
a: wget -o wget.log http://@host@/file.txt
why: fixture-cost
ref: wget
teach: wget -o
hint: -o redirects wget's log messages into a file
---
q: {fetch https://@host@/file.txt uncertified with wget -- know why|skip cert checks with wget fetching https://@host@/file.txt|pull https://@host@/file.txt via wget, cert check off, risky}
a: wget --no-check-certificate https://@host@/file.txt
why: fixture-cost
ref: wget
teach: wget --no-check-certificate
hint: this turns off the only thing proving you are talking to who you think
---
q: {see what's listening on @host@:@port@ with a raw connection|poke whatever's on @host@:@port@ directly|probe @host@:@port@ with a bare tcp connection}
a: nc @host@ @port@
why: live-net
ref: nc
teach: nc
hint: nc with just a host and port opens a raw tcp connection
---
q: {send a HEAD request to @host@ on port 80 via nc, by hand|hand-craft a HEAD request to @host@ port 80, send it through nc|pipe a HEAD request into nc, talking http to @host@ port 80}
a: printf 'HEAD / HTTP/1.0\r\n\r\n' | nc @host@ 80
why: live-net
ref: nc
teach: nc
hint: pipe a raw request into nc and read the raw response back
---
setup: Telnet still opens gear too old for ssh -- no encryption, but
setup: sometimes the only door in.
q: {reach a telnet-only box at @host@ on port @port@|open a session to old gear at @host@:@port@ that only speaks telnet|connect to a device at @host@:@port@ that has no ssh, only telnet}
a: telnet @host@ @port@
why: live-net
ref: telnet
teach: telnet
hint: telnet HOST PORT opens a plain tcp session to that port
---
q: {log into @host@ over telnet as @user@, skip the prompt|telnet into @host@, logging straight in as @user@|reach @host@ over telnet already logged in as @user@}
a: telnet -l @user@ @host@
why: live-net
ref: telnet
teach: telnet -l
hint: -l sends a login name as soon as the connection opens
---
setup: tftp has no login and no encryption -- exactly what firmware on
setup: embedded gear still expects. The remote file is file.txt throughout.
q: {fetch a file over tftp from @host@|download a file using tftp from @host@|use tftp to fetch a file from @host@}
a: tftp -g -r file.txt @host@
why: live-net
ref: tftp
teach: tftp -g
teach: tftp -r
hint: -g gets a file, -r names which one
---
q: {upload @txt@ over tftp to @host@|send @txt@ up over tftp to @host@|use tftp to upload @txt@ to @host@}
a: tftp -p -l @txt@ @host@
why: live-net
ref: tftp
teach: tftp -p
teach: tftp -l
hint: -p puts a file, -l names the local file to send
---
setup: ftpget and ftpput both name the local file local.txt and the remote
setup: one remote.txt, and the password is secret. Only the flag changes.
q: {fetch a file from the ftp server @host@|download a file from the ftp server @host@|use ftpget to pull a file from @host@}
a: ftpget @host@ local.txt remote.txt
why: live-net
ref: ftpget
teach: ftpget
hint: ftpget takes the local name then the remote name, in that order
---
setup: local.txt and remote.txt are the names throughout, and secret is the password.
q: {fetch from @host@ via ftpget as @user@, with a password|download from @host@ via ftpget, authenticating as @user@|use ftpget with the username @user@ to fetch from @host@}
a: ftpget -u @user@ -p secret @host@ local.txt remote.txt
why: live-net
ref: ftpget
teach: ftpget -u
teach: ftpget -p
hint: -u sets the username, -p the password
---
setup: local.txt and remote.txt are the names throughout.
q: {fetch from @host@ on the non-standard ftpget port @port@|download via ftpget from @host@ listening on port @port@|use ftpget against @host@ on the non-default port @port@}
a: ftpget -P @port@ @host@ local.txt remote.txt
why: live-net
ref: ftpget
teach: ftpget -P
hint: -P picks the port instead of ftp's default 21
---
setup: local.txt and remote.txt are the names throughout.
q: {continue an interrupted ftpget download from @host@|resume an ftpget download from @host@ that got interrupted|pick an interrupted ftpget download from @host@ back up}
a: ftpget -c @host@ local.txt remote.txt
why: live-net
ref: ftpget
teach: ftpget -c
hint: -c continues a download from where it stopped
---
setup: remote.txt is the name @txt@ takes on the server.
q: {put @txt@ up on the ftp server @host@|send @txt@ up to the ftp server @host@|use ftpput to upload @txt@ to @host@}
a: ftpput @host@ remote.txt @txt@
why: live-net
ref: ftpput
teach: ftpput
hint: ftpput takes the remote name then the local name, in that order
---
setup: remote.txt is the name @txt@ takes on the server, and secret is the password.
q: {upload @txt@ to @host@'s ftpput, authenticating as @user@|send @txt@ to @host@ via ftpput, logged in as @user@|use ftpput as @user@ to upload @txt@ to @host@}
a: ftpput -u @user@ -p secret @host@ remote.txt @txt@
why: live-net
ref: ftpput
teach: ftpput -u
teach: ftpput -p
hint: -u sets the username, -p the password, same as ftpget
---
setup: remote.txt is the name @txt@ takes on the server.
q: {upload @txt@ to @host@ on ftpput port @port@|send @txt@ to @host@'s ftpput on port @port@|use ftpput against @host@:@port@ to send @txt@}
a: ftpput -P @port@ @host@ remote.txt @txt@
why: live-net
ref: ftpput
teach: ftpput -P
hint: -P picks the port instead of ftp's default 21
---
setup: Nothing routes anywhere until an interface has an address -- ask
setup: the network for one.
q: {ask for a dhcp lease on @iface@ with udhcpc|request a dhcp lease on @iface@ with udhcpc|use udhcpc to get a lease on @iface@}
a: udhcpc -i @iface@
why: live-net
ref: udhcpc
teach: udhcpc -i
hint: -i names which interface to request a lease on
---
q: {get a udhcpc lease on @iface@ and exit rather than staying resident|grab a udhcpc lease on @iface@ then quit, instead of staying resident|request a udhcpc lease on @iface@ and exit immediately, no daemon}
a: udhcpc -i @iface@ -q
why: live-net
ref: udhcpc
teach: udhcpc -q
hint: -q exits once a lease is obtained instead of staying resident
---
q: {watch udhcpc negotiate a lease on @iface@ instead of it backgrounding|keep udhcpc on @iface@ in front of you instead of letting it daemonize|run udhcpc on @iface@ so you can watch it work, not backgrounded}
a: udhcpc -i @iface@ -f
why: live-net
ref: udhcpc
teach: udhcpc -f
hint: -f keeps udhcpc in the foreground instead of daemonizing
---
q: {exit rather than keep retrying udhcpc on @iface@ if no lease arrives|give up and exit if udhcpc gets no lease ever on @iface@|stop instead of retrying udhcpc on @iface@ forever, no lease showing}
a: udhcpc -i @iface@ -n
why: live-net
ref: udhcpc
teach: udhcpc -n
hint: -n exits immediately if no lease shows up, rather than retrying forever
---
q: {run /usr/share/udhcpc/default.script on @iface@ once the lease arrives|name /usr/share/udhcpc/default.script to run when @iface@ gets a lease|have @iface@'s lease run /usr/share/udhcpc/default.script}
a: udhcpc -i @iface@ -s /usr/share/udhcpc/default.script
why: live-net
ref: udhcpc
teach: udhcpc -s
hint: -s names a script to run once the lease is obtained
